ction in Example 6. (b) The graph of the function in Example 6. The blue points are the candidates for maxima or minima."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.88938,"w":0.59307,"h":0.04497,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Solving extreme value problems with algebraic constraints on the variables usually requires the method of Lagrange multipliers introduced in the next section. But sometimes we can solve such problems directly, as in the next example."}]}